To generate permutations of a specific size in C++, you can use two loops. Here's an example implementation:

```cpp
template <typename F, typename T>
void permutation(F f, std::vector<T> v, std::size_t n)
{
    std::vector<bool> bs(v.size() - n, false);
    bs.resize(v.size(), true);
    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());

    do {
        std::vector<T> sub;
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i != bs.size(); ++i) {
            if (bs[i]) {
                sub.push_back(v[i]);
            }
        }
        do {
            f(sub);
        } while (std::next_permutation(sub.begin(), sub.end()));
    } while (std::next_permutation(bs.begin(), bs.end()));
}
```

You can use this template function `permutation()` to generate the desired permutations. It takes three parameters:
- `f`: A function or lambda that will be called with each generated permutation as an argument. You can define this function to perform any desired action with the permutation.
- `v`: The vector of objects for which permutations need to be generated.
- `n`: The size of the permutations.

Here's an example usage of the `permutation()` function:

```cpp
std::vector<std::pair<unsigned long, unsigned long>> vec = { {1, 2}, {3, 4}, {5, 6} };

permutation([](const std::vector<std::pair<unsigned long, unsigned long>>& perm){
