You can implement a stack in Java by creating a `Stack` class with the necessary methods. Here is an example implementation:

```java
public class Stack {

    private int top;
    private int[] stack; 
    private int size; 

    // constructor
    public Stack(int size) {
        this.size = size;
        this.stack = new int[size];
        this.top = -1;
    }

    public boolean isFull() {
        return (top == size - 1);
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return (top == -1);    
    }

    public void push(int x) {
        if (isFull()) {
            System.out.println("Stack is full!");
        } else {
            stack[++top] = x;
        }
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            return -1;
        } else {
            return stack[top--];
        }
    }
}
```

In this implementation, we have a private variable `top` to keep track of the top element in the stack, an array `stack` to store the stack elements, and a variable `size` to specify the maximum size of the stack.

The `isFull()` method checks if the stack is full by comparing the `top` variable with `size - 1`. If the `top` is equal to `size - 1`, it means the stack is full.

The `isEmpty()` method checks if the stack is empty by checking if the `top` is equal to -1. If the `top` is -1, it means the stack is empty.

The `push(int x)` method adds an element `x` to the stack. It first checks if the stack is full using the `isFull()` method. If the stack is full, it prints a message "Stack is full!" to indicate that the stack is already full. Otherwise, it increments `top` by 1 and assigns the value of `x` to `stack[top]`.

The `pop()` method removes and returns the top element from the stack. It first checks if the stack is empty using the `isEmpty()` method. If the stack is empty, it returns -1 to indicate that the stack is already empty. Otherwise, it decrements `top` by 1 and returns the value of `stack[top]`.

