One way to solve the longest common subsequence problem is by using the dynamic programming approach. Here's an algorithm that you can use:

```
int LCS(string X, string Y, int m, int n) 
{ 
    int L[m + 1][n + 1]; 

    for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) 
    { 
        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) 
        { 
            if (i == 0 || j == 0) 
                L[i][j] = 0; 
            else if (X[i - 1] == Y[j - 1]) 
                L[i][j] = L[i - 1][j - 1] + 1; 
            else
                L[i][j] = max(L[i - 1][j], L[i][j - 1]); 
        } 
    } 

    return L[m][n]; 
}
```

In this algorithm, `X` and `Y` are the two strings for which we want to find the longest common subsequence. `m` and `n` represent the lengths of `X` and `Y`, respectively. The algorithm uses a 2D array `L` to store the lengths of the common subsequences of the prefixes of `X` and `Y`.

The algorithm iterates through the lengths of prefixes, starting from 0 to `m` and 0 to `n`. If the characters at the current positions in `X` and `Y` are the same, the algorithm increments the length of the common subsequence by 1. Otherwise, it takes the maximum length from either the prefix of `X` or the prefix of `Y`.

Finally, the algorithm returns the length of the longest common subsequence, which is stored in `L[m][n]`.

