You can check whether a specific bit is `1` or `0` by using a bitwise AND operation. This is done by creating a mask with the bit you want to check set to `1` and all other bits set to `0`. Then you perform a bitwise AND operation between the byte and the mask. If the result is `0`, it means that the bit you checked was `0`, and if it's not `0`, it means that the bit you checked was `1`.

Let's assume 'n' is the position of the bit you want to check, starting from the least significant bit at position 0, and 'byte' is the byte you want to check.

Here's an example in Java:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        byte myByte = (byte) 0b10101010;  // Your byte
        int n = 2;  // Position of the bit you want to check

        // Create the mask
        byte mask = (byte) (1 << n);

        // Perform the bitwise AND operation
        byte result = (byte) (myByte & mask);

        if (result == 0) {
            System.out.println("Bit is 0");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Bit is 1");
        }
    }
}
```

If you want to check multiple bits, you can adjust the mask to have those bits set to `1`.

Note: The bitwise AND operation `&` compares each bit of the first operand to the corresponding bit of the second operand. If both bits are `1`, the corresponding result bit is set to `1`. Otherwise, the corresponding result bit is set to `0`.

String interning is a method of storing only one copy of each distinct string value, which must be immutable. The string interning pool is used to reduce the memory usage when you have many instances of the same string. 

In the .NET Framework, string interning isn't automatically applied when you concatenate strings or build them using StringBuilder. When you concatenate strings or build them using StringBuilder, a new string object is created in memory to hold the resulting string. This new string object is not in the intern pool, unless you explicitly add it with the String.Intern method. 

So in simple words, string concatenation or using StringBuilder doesn't change anything for the interned strings, they don't put the new string into intern pool either, unless explicitly asked to do so.

For instance:

string s = new StringBuilder().Append("Hello").Append(" ").Append("World").ToString();

// s is not added in the string intern pool

string poolString = string.Intern(s);

// Now s is added in the string intern pool

When you call String.Intern, it checks whether the string you are passing as an argument is already in the intern pool. If it's there, it returns a reference to that string. If it's not there, it adds the string to the pool and then returns a reference to it. This explicit call to intern is rare, but could be used in scenarios where you know you'll be using the same string repetitively and you want to save memory.

Remember that interning strings can be costly (in terms of time) so it should be done when the benefits clearly outweight the costs. In many cases, relying on the .NET runtime to manage memory for you (without interning strings) is sufficient and leads to cleaner, simpler code.

Here is an implementation of a hybrid Mergesort-Insertion algorithm in Java:

```java
class HybridSort {
    int THRESHOLD = 5;

    void hybridSort(int[] array, int l, int h) {
        if (l < h) {
            if (h - l <= THRESHOLD) {
                insertionSort(array, l, h);
            } else {
                int mid = (l + h) / 2;
                hybridSort(array, l, mid);
                hybridSort(array, mid + 1, h);
                merge(array, l, mid, h);
            }
        }
    }

    void merge(int[] array, int l, int mid, int h) {
        int len1 = mid - l + 1, len2 = h - mid;
        int[] left = new int[len1];
        int[] right = new int[len2];
        System.arraycopy(array, l, left, 0, len1);
        System.arraycopy(array, mid + 1, right, 0, len2);
        int i = 0, j = 0;
        while (i < len1 && j < len2) {
            if (left[i] <= right[j]) {
                array[l++] = left[i++];
            } else {
                array[l++] = right[j++];
            }
        }
        while (i < len1) {
            array[l++] = left[i++];
        }
        while (j < len2) {
            array[l++] = right[j++];
        }
    }

    void insertionSort(int[] array, int l, int h) {
        for (int i = l + 1; i <= h; i++) {
            int key = array[i];
            int j = i - 1;
            while (j >= l && array[j] > key) {
                array[j + 1] = array[j];
                j--;
            }
            array[j + 1] = key;
        }
    }
}
```

Handle Memory Overflow: 
The above implementation could result in a StackOverflowError for arrays that are exceptionally large. You should consider using a bottom-up Mergesort implementation (iterative process instead of recursive), or using a language/infrastructure that supports tail call optimization. Using a threshold for switching to insertion sort also reduces recursive calls.

Complexity Analysis:
The time complexity of Mergesort is O(n log n). The insertion Sort algorithm has a linear runtime in best case scenario i.e., O(n) and quadratic otherwise i.e., O(n^2). By utilizing Insertion sort for small subarrays, we can improve the efficiency of Mergesort, reducing the constant factors in the Mergesort time complexity. However, the worst-case time complexity remains O(n log n). 

The space complexity of Mergesort is O(n) because the auxiliary space needed is proportional to the number of elements in the array. If space complexity is a concern, an in-place version of Mergesort could be used, which has a space complexity of O(1), but has complex implementation and higher time complexity.

