This behavior is due to React's "Batching". When you are setting state and immediately trying to log it, you might not get new state value because state updates might be asynchronous for performance reasons. 

In your example, you are trying to log the updated value immediately after updating it. However, due to the asynchronous nature, the update hasn't been committed to the state when the `alert(data)` line executes. As a result, it is displaying old state value (which is "ammad"). 

What you are seeing in console (or in the rendered DOM) is the result of React's re-render that happens after the state update is applied and the component is re-rendered. This is why you see the updated value after the update. 

If you want to track the state update, you can use useEffect Hook. 

```
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function App() {

  let [data,setdata]=useState("ammad")

  useEffect(() => {
    alert(data);
  }, [data]);

  function apple() {
    setdata("Hi, I am ammad jahangir")
  }

  console.warn(data)

  return (
    <div className="App">
      <h1>{data}</h1>

      <button onClick={apple}>Click me</button>
    </div>
  );
